WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.430
Inference and significance

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.700
What does 95% confidence actually mean?

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:24.720
The method captures the true value 19 times in 20 across repeated sampling. It is not a 95% chance the truth is in this interval — the truth is fixed. Formula: estimate ± 1.96 × standard error.

4
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.250
Hypothesis testing in four steps?

5
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:38.570
State the null. Assume it true. Ask how likely a relationship this big is by sampling luck — that is p. If p &lt; .05, reject the null. You never accept it.

6
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.340
Type I vs. Type II error?

7
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:54.830
Type I is a false positive — you announced something that is not there; p &lt; .05 caps that risk at 5%. Type II is a false negative, usually from too small a sample.

8
00:00:54.830 --> 00:01:00.500
Significant at p &lt; .001 — is it important?

9
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:16.100
Unknown. With 300,000 cases a tenth of a point clears p &lt; .05 and means nothing. Levitt’s capital-punishment estimate explains about 1.5% of the 1990s homicide decline: possibly real, definitely not the answer.

10
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.150
What does significance say about causality?

11
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:28.110
Nothing. A significant correlation still has to clear all four hurdles. This is the mistake that costs the most points.

12
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.000
Inference and significance — 5 cards
